WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.936 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:07.380 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:17.060 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.320 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: So I'll talk
mostly about risk-neutral

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.080 align:middle line:84%
pricing, and then Black-Scholes
will be more of an illustration

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.600 align:middle line:90%
to the method--

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.920 align:middle line:84%
to more general
method, which I think

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.920 align:middle line:84%
is very important,
and very important

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.960 align:middle line:90%
for pricing derivatives.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.440 align:middle line:84%
And a few of you did
look into Black-Scholes.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.280 align:middle line:84%
So my hope is to give a little
bit more depth and a little bit

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.480 align:middle line:84%
more perspective on
what's the bigger

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.200 align:middle line:84%
picture of derivative pricing
through risk-neutral valuation.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:07.780 align:middle line:84%
But as promised, before, let's
start from the interest rates

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.620 align:middle line:90%
from the slide which--

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.420 align:middle line:84%
similar slide which I showed
you on the first class.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And Andrew Gunderson
already talked about

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.520 align:middle line:84%
yield curve construction
and everything.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:24.980 align:middle line:84%
But, yeah, so that's what
happened since we started.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.140 align:middle line:90%
That's the yield curve--

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.740 align:middle line:84%
or it's just a splitting,
as it was in September

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.400 align:middle line:84%
just before beginning
of the class,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.860 align:middle line:90%
and that's where we are now.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.620 align:middle line:84%
So quite predictable,
in a sense,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:46.380 align:middle line:84%
because Fed moved the
rates by 75 basis points.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.430 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what happened here.

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.300 align:middle line:90%


00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:06.710 align:middle line:84%
The parallel shift is
only at 70 basis points

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.220 align:middle line:90%
here, but more of a 50 here.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.070 align:middle line:90%


00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:17.150 align:middle line:90%
Now, a little bit of heuristics.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.990 align:middle line:90%
How does it look like?

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, right now,
it looks like we

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:31.990 align:middle line:84%
are very similar to this
shape, which was the 19.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:38.070 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand,
this shape is almost

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:44.390 align:middle line:84%
exactly the same, which was, in
fact, just before the crisis.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.970 align:middle line:90%
So, in both cases, a recession--

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in both cases, there,
the curves are inverted,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.170 align:middle line:84%
and in both cases, a recession
followed shortly after.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:03:00.370 align:middle line:84%
I certainly think-- hope that it
is closer to this because just--

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:05.530 align:middle line:84%
this is basically the next
snapshot in '21, and the curve

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.450 align:middle line:84%
is completely normal shape,
and the economy was--

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.370 align:middle line:84%
the recession was very
short and the economy

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.490 align:middle line:84%
was on a very solid
track by then.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.890 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully we are
not in this situation

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.470 align:middle line:84%
because this is just
before the crisis.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.950 align:middle line:84%
It's before Bear
Stearns collapsed,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.050 align:middle line:84%
before Lehman collapsed,
so a lot of bad things

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.410 align:middle line:90%
happened after that.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:36.590 align:middle line:84%
In any case, this is all
non-scientific prediction,

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:42.890 align:middle line:84%
and the real question here would
be to do some thorough data

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.210 align:middle line:90%
analysis, something like--

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.650 align:middle line:84%
Stefan Andreev talked
about analyzing

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.490 align:middle line:84%
the shape of the
curves and bond markets

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:58.180 align:middle line:84%
using a PCA, Principal
Component Analysis.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.260 align:middle line:84%
But always fascinating
to look at the data

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.300 align:middle line:84%
and always fascinating to
look at the curves, which,

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.780 align:middle line:84%
in my opinion, are
in the middle--

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.300 align:middle line:90%
in the center of finance.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:21.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's move on to
the topic of our class.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:28.140 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is to see
how replicating the payoff

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.500 align:middle line:84%
can help us to
price derivatives.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:38.060 align:middle line:84%
And how to learn
that actually, if we

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.980 align:middle line:84%
can follow market dynamics,
how it can help us

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.380 align:middle line:90%
to price derivatives.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.300 align:middle line:84%
And actually, that the
real-world dynamics

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.680 align:middle line:90%
is not that important.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.320 align:middle line:90%
It's what market implies.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.440 align:middle line:90%
That's what important.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.080 align:middle line:84%
And how it is connected
to martingales

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.540 align:middle line:84%
and to mathematical apparatus,
to stochastic calculus

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.220 align:middle line:90%
connecting that.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.480 align:middle line:90%
But here is a simple example.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:08.230 align:middle line:90%
Horse betting.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:10.760 align:middle line:90%


00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:17.280 align:middle line:90%
So let's assume-- it is clear--

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.540 align:middle line:84%
that we have two horses,
Horse 1 and Horse 2.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.600 align:middle line:90%


00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.480 align:middle line:84%
And we-- I mean,
you're a bookie,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.220 align:middle line:90%
and you know the horse as well.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:34.040 align:middle line:84%
You know that this horse
has 20% to chance to win

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:38.080 align:middle line:84%
and this horse has
80% chance of winning.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.760 align:middle line:84%
But people bet,
bet on the market,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:49.700 align:middle line:90%
and 10,000 is bet on this horse.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.680 align:middle line:84%
People don't know exactly
these probabilities.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.780 align:middle line:84%
They know that this horse
is better than this.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:04.380 align:middle line:84%
So they bet-- rather, the public
bets 50,000 on this and 10,000

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.735 align:middle line:90%
on this.

00:06:07.735 --> 00:06:12.700 align:middle line:84%
And what you do, you
just take a commission.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.140 align:middle line:90%
Say 1%.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:20.820 align:middle line:90%
And you have 6,000 in your--

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.180 align:middle line:84%
600 in your pocket
of the commission.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.340 align:middle line:84%
So now the question is, you need
to set the odds for this race.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.540 align:middle line:84%
So, well, one way
to set the odds

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:36.560 align:middle line:84%
is knowing the real
exact probabilities.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.440 align:middle line:84%
One, it's reasonable to
say, let's make it 4 to 1.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:46.860 align:middle line:90%


00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.730 align:middle line:90%
So what outcomes are possible?

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:54.470 align:middle line:90%
Then horse starts, horses race.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:59.140 align:middle line:90%
So, H1 wins, H2 wins.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.710 align:middle line:90%


00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.630 align:middle line:90%
So if a horse--

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:14.770 align:middle line:84%
the first horse wins, you
will get 50,000, this amount,

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:21.070 align:middle line:84%
and you will pay out 4 times
this amount because you set

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:22.610 align:middle line:90%
the odds 4 to 1.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:28.350 align:middle line:90%


00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:36.430 align:middle line:84%
So, this will mean that you
will be left with 10,000 profit.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.550 align:middle line:90%
This is low-probability outcome.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.010 align:middle line:84%
You know that second
horse is better.

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.350 align:middle line:84%
So what happens if
second horse wins?

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.390 align:middle line:90%
Well, you will keep this money.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:52.890 align:middle line:90%
So it will be 10,000.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.130 align:middle line:84%
And you will pay
a quarter of this.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:08:03.170 align:middle line:90%


00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:14.310 align:middle line:84%
And this will be, on my
books, minus 2,500 at the end.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.650 align:middle line:84%
So you will lose,
you will have to pay.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:21.890 align:middle line:84%
Of course, if you compute
the expected value,

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:27.650 align:middle line:84%
if horses run many, many
times, it will be 0.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:31.770 align:middle line:90%
10 times 20.2 plus--

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:37.150 align:middle line:84%
minus 2, 2 and 1/2
times 0.8 is 0.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:44.835 align:middle line:84%
But for this, on average,
the horses have to run a lot.

00:08:44.835 --> 00:08:51.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's consider a
different way of setting odds.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.920 align:middle line:90%
You forget about these numbers.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.700 align:middle line:90%
You just look at what was bad.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.320 align:middle line:84%
And you rather set
the odds 5 to 1.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:05.780 align:middle line:90%


00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:09.060 align:middle line:90%
OK, let's see what happens.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:16.696 align:middle line:84%
Well, if the first horse
wins, you will get 500--

00:09:16.696 --> 00:09:27.100 align:middle line:84%
or 50,000, and
you'll pay 50,000.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:34.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, if second horse
wins, you'll get 10,000,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:42.960 align:middle line:84%
and one-fifth of this,
so you pay out 10,000.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:50.000 align:middle line:84%
So no matter which horse
wins, you break even for sure

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.640 align:middle line:90%
in this particular outcome.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:56.160 align:middle line:84%
And if you were collecting some
fee, you just keep the fee,

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.360 align:middle line:90%
you would do it next time.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:08.480 align:middle line:84%
So, basically what you did
by setting the odds according

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.960 align:middle line:84%
to the market, you
hedge yourself.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:18.160 align:middle line:84%
You eliminated every
uncertainty, which is probably

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.600 align:middle line:90%
the outcome which you want.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.820 align:middle line:84%
All right, so this
is all about betting,

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.480 align:middle line:90%
and we are not betting people.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.560 align:middle line:90%
In fact, we are scientists.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:38.720 align:middle line:84%
And we'll be talking today
about pricing derivatives.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.440 align:middle line:84%
Again, some simple
examples and derivatives

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:47.460 align:middle line:84%
which we will be looking
at will be call/put options

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.840 align:middle line:90%
and forward contracts.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.120 align:middle line:84%
Just to refresh,
so what are those?

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:57.500 align:middle line:84%
So the simplest
possible derivative

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.620 align:middle line:84%
is a forward contract,
which is an agreement

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:03.500 align:middle line:84%
to buy a security--
in our case, a stock,

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.670 align:middle line:84%
in the future for a
price which is set today.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.220 align:middle line:90%


00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:14.340 align:middle line:84%
Generally speaking-- so
the payout of a forward

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:24.740 align:middle line:84%
is just the difference
of the strike

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:33.220 align:middle line:84%
and the price at the
expiration-- at the expiry.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:40.150 align:middle line:84%
So the payout is S minus
K, where K is a strike.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:41.490 align:middle line:90%
Agreed price.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:45.830 align:middle line:84%
And for forwards,
the agreement is

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:49.550 align:middle line:84%
that the strike is
set in such a way

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.070 align:middle line:84%
that it costs you nothing
to enter it today.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:58.830 align:middle line:84%
You basically say, we agree
with you today for a zero price

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:07.190 align:middle line:84%
that I'll buy the
stock at the price K.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:12.110 align:middle line:84%
So quite a useful
diagram is the payout.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:17.710 align:middle line:84%
That's-- sorry, that's
the blue line, so it's--

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.200 align:middle line:84%
in our case, it's S minus
K. So that's forward.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.990 align:middle line:90%


00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.230 align:middle line:90%
Now, call option.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:34.590 align:middle line:84%
Call option is an option to
buy a stock at price K. So

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:40.650 align:middle line:84%
basically, at expiry,
you will decide,

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.400 align:middle line:84%
are you buying the
stock at price K?

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.370 align:middle line:90%


00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.450 align:middle line:84%
Or if you don't
want to, you won't.

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:55.450 align:middle line:84%
And generally speaking, if
the stock ends at the money,

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.070 align:middle line:84%
higher than the strike,
you will buy it,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:12:59.330 align:middle line:90%
because it will be cheaper.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:06.730 align:middle line:84%
and if the stock ends
below the strike price,

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.610 align:middle line:84%
you'll say, thank you very
much, and the contract

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.130 align:middle line:90%
will expire worthless.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:15.330 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
payout at the end.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:19.150 align:middle line:84%
And this is the price of
the call option today.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.150 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, it is not
zero-price contract.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:27.140 align:middle line:84%
It is-- you will pay some money
to enter into this contract.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:32.330 align:middle line:90%


00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:36.270 align:middle line:84%
Another usual and
simple contract

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:45.990 align:middle line:84%
would be a put option, which
is an agreement to sell the--

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:47.590 align:middle line:90%
it's kind of opposite right.

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:55.230 align:middle line:84%
It's basically prevents--
if the option--

00:13:55.230 --> 00:14:00.050 align:middle line:84%
the stock is too high,
you won't sell it.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:05.470 align:middle line:84%
If the stock ends low, you will
sell it for a higher price.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:14.270 align:middle line:84%
So it is basically insurance,
insurance against stock

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:18.110 align:middle line:90%
falling below the strike price.

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.910 align:middle line:90%
And here is the current price.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:26.330 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll be doing today
is deriving these curves,

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:28.830 align:middle line:84%
deriving the current
price, the price today,

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:32.070 align:middle line:84%
for the options
and the forwards.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:45.240 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see is a few
observations that the only--

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:48.440 align:middle line:84%
what will matter,
it is assumptions

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:54.960 align:middle line:84%
on the dynamics of the stock
price, some assumptions on how

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.230 align:middle line:84%
the stock behaves, some
assumptions on interest rates.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.840 align:middle line:90%


00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:07.040 align:middle line:84%
And given that, there
will be no uncertainty

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.580 align:middle line:90%
in the price of the derivative.

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:10.840 align:middle line:84%
And this was-- believe
me, this wasn't

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:14.290 align:middle line:84%
clear to market participants
before Black and Scholes.

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:21.560 align:middle line:90%


00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:31.540 align:middle line:84%
And the other
important observation

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:35.700 align:middle line:84%
which we'll see, that
the preferences of market

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.400 align:middle line:90%
participants will not matter.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:41.900 align:middle line:84%
It's in a similarly to
our horse-racing example,

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:49.860 align:middle line:84%
we'll be able to find the
riskless ways to replicate

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:53.860 align:middle line:90%
the price of the option.

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:54.980 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:16:04.300 align:middle line:84%
So, let's start with a simple
example of the forward.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:11.700 align:middle line:84%
And let's start from a
discrete time and stock prices

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:16.300 align:middle line:84%
because it's simpler
to understand.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:19.720 align:middle line:90%
And so what does it mean?

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:28.630 align:middle line:84%
Is that right now, we have
some price of our stock as 0.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:31.550 align:middle line:90%


00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:37.370 align:middle line:84%
We have price of
our contract, F0.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:40.510 align:middle line:84%
In the case of the
forward, we assume

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:45.950 align:middle line:84%
that it's 0 because
we assume that we

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:49.430 align:middle line:84%
are entering at the forward
contract at 0 price.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:55.870 align:middle line:84%
And there are two outcomes
that we are only one step away.

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:59.630 align:middle line:90%
So some time dt to the future.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:05.750 align:middle line:84%
And at the outcomes, either
S1 or S2, so the stock

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.550 align:middle line:90%
can go either here and here.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:13.109 align:middle line:84%
And our derivative,
according to our formula,

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:27.954 align:middle line:84%
will go to S1 minus
K or S2 minus K.

00:17:27.954 --> 00:17:31.330 align:middle line:84%
So, again, from
naive perspective,

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:37.010 align:middle line:84%
one can say, well, there is some
probability for stock to go up

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:44.250 align:middle line:84%
to S1 and some probability of
1 minus P for stock to go down.

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:50.210 align:middle line:84%
And one would say that we
can assume that our price now

00:17:50.210 --> 00:18:02.330 align:middle line:84%
is actually expected value with
this probability of the value

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.600 align:middle line:90%
of our derivative at the end.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:08.010 align:middle line:90%


00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:19.720 align:middle line:84%
And so it will be P times
F1 minus 1 minus P times F2.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:23.850 align:middle line:90%


00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And-- well, P times S1 minus K
plus 1 minus P times S2 minus K.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:46.590 align:middle line:90%
And from here, we can actually--

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:50.850 align:middle line:84%
what we are looking for is the
agreed price on our forward.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:54.990 align:middle line:84%
So we can say that our
strike price should

00:18:54.990 --> 00:19:08.670 align:middle line:90%
be PS1 plus 1 minus P times S2.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:11.670 align:middle line:84%
But we don't know
the probability,

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.730 align:middle line:84%
and in fact, that's, as
we'll see in a second,

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:20.630 align:middle line:84%
it's not a good
way to approach it.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:21.350 align:middle line:90%
And why?

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:23.310 align:middle line:90%
Here is why.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.480 align:middle line:84%
Because here is a suggestion
what we should do.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:30.400 align:middle line:84%
Here is a suggestion
how we can construct

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:33.440 align:middle line:90%
a risk-neutral portfolio here.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:38.400 align:middle line:84%
So, that's what we
are going to do.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:46.780 align:middle line:84%
So at time 0, we enter into
forward contract with strike K,

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:52.190 align:middle line:84%
and in a second, we'll see what
the strike K will be, at 0 cost.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:55.720 align:middle line:90%


00:19:55.720 --> 00:20:03.010 align:middle line:90%
Enter forward at 0 cost.

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:06.080 align:middle line:90%


00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:11.240 align:middle line:84%
And we will borrow
money to buy the stock.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:19.100 align:middle line:90%
So we're borrow F0.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:27.540 align:middle line:84%
So we'll borrow money in
a bank and buy the stock.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:30.220 align:middle line:84%
For a minute, let's assume
that our interest rates

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:32.950 align:middle line:90%
are 0 for simplicity.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:33.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:35.080 align:middle line:90%
So what happens at expiry?

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.140 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:40.910 align:middle line:84%
It's time equals
0, time equal dt.

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:44.100 align:middle line:90%


00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:52.860 align:middle line:84%
So at expiry, what we'll do,
we will deliver the stock

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:56.100 align:middle line:90%
because we have it.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:04.110 align:middle line:84%
Deliver S. And we will
receive our strike price.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:14.260 align:middle line:90%


00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:15.800 align:middle line:90%
And repay the loan.

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:20.830 align:middle line:90%


00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.590 align:middle line:90%
So basically, what happens here?

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.770 align:middle line:90%
We borrow here, we had a stock.

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:29.410 align:middle line:84%
We came here, we delivered
the stock, this costs nothing.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:34.310 align:middle line:84%
So we receive K.
That's agreed strike.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:38.110 align:middle line:84%
And we repay the loan,
which we started here.

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:42.570 align:middle line:84%
Because interest rate
is 0, It didn't change.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:46.790 align:middle line:84%
If it changed, it will
be some e to the minus--

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:50.510 align:middle line:90%
e to the rt times F0.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:56.230 align:middle line:84%
So, now, that's
what we'll get here.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:22:01.790 align:middle line:84%
And here, we started with 0
because we paid 0 to enter.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:05.150 align:middle line:90%
What can we say about this?

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:13.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this was greater than
0, we for sure receive money.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:16.150 align:middle line:90%
There is no other outcome.

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:20.170 align:middle line:84%
If it's less than 0,
for sure lost money.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:26.250 align:middle line:84%
No arbitrage, assume will tell
us that neither is possible.

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:29.690 align:middle line:84%
So because the price
was-- initial price was 0,

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:32.050 align:middle line:90%
this has to be 0.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:35.250 align:middle line:84%
Because this
replicates our payoff.

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:40.930 align:middle line:90%
So our strike has to be 0.

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:43.570 align:middle line:84%
So if there are
no interest rates,

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:46.490 align:middle line:84%
the forward contract
would just--

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:51.370 align:middle line:84%
we would agree to buy
or sell the price--

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.530 align:middle line:84%
the stock in the future
at the current price

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:55.800 align:middle line:90%
if interest rates are 0.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:59.650 align:middle line:90%


00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:05.810 align:middle line:84%
So no choice, and we don't care
about real-world probabilities,

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:15.610 align:middle line:84%
and we don't care about how
the stock behaves in real life.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:17.750 align:middle line:90%
We just replicated everything.

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:20.030 align:middle line:84%
What's interesting,
though, what's interesting,

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:25.150 align:middle line:84%
though, we can find
the probability, which

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:31.990 align:middle line:84%
will give us our strike-- or
which will give us this formula.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.530 align:middle line:90%
So what will it be?

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:37.590 align:middle line:90%
Well, well K is equal F0.

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:42.350 align:middle line:90%


00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:48.750 align:middle line:84%
And so our P-- and
I'll call it neutral

00:23:48.750 --> 00:24:00.090 align:middle line:84%
risk-- neutral probability,
S1 plus 1 minus Pn S2.

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:04.830 align:middle line:90%


00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:11.120 align:middle line:84%
And we can find
our Pn from here.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:15.840 align:middle line:90%


00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:21.410 align:middle line:84%
S0 minus S2 divided
by S1 minus S2.

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:27.560 align:middle line:90%


00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:30.000 align:middle line:84%
What's interesting observation
here, well, first of all,

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.240 align:middle line:84%
that we can find this
risk-neutral probabilities.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:39.320 align:middle line:84%
And we actually could
price our forward contract

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:44.320 align:middle line:84%
using these probabilities,
using this expected payoff.

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.720 align:middle line:84%
The interesting-- the
most interesting part

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:53.240 align:middle line:84%
is that our current stock
price our underlying

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.560 align:middle line:84%
under this risk-neutral
probability

00:24:55.560 --> 00:25:00.400 align:middle line:84%
is expected value of the
future move of the stock.

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:01.820 align:middle line:90%
And that's what we will see.

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:05.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's basically our
risk-neutral measure.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And that's how we
will be looking

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:11.360 align:middle line:84%
for our risk-neutral measure
because we can always

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:18.460 align:middle line:84%
find a measure which implies
that our current underlying

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:24.360 align:middle line:84%
value, asset value, is expected
value of the future payoff.

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:25.980 align:middle line:84%
And that's a
measure which we can

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:30.380 align:middle line:90%
use to price the derivatives.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:31.140 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:35.620 align:middle line:84%
So let's move to something
more interesting.

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:45.940 align:middle line:84%
And let's do a call option,
but still in discrete time.

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:56.700 align:middle line:90%


00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:02.700 align:middle line:84%
So, our call option
payoff would be

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:13.120 align:middle line:84%
maximum of the value at
expiration minus K and 0.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:18.550 align:middle line:90%


00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:19.450 align:middle line:90%
That's here.

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:21.850 align:middle line:90%
That's our payoff.

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:24.030 align:middle line:90%
That's the hockey stick.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:28.810 align:middle line:84%
But we still assume,
for this exercise,

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:32.550 align:middle line:84%
let's still assume
interest rates are 0.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:38.710 align:middle line:84%
So our diagram will be our value
of call option, which we'll

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:45.590 align:middle line:84%
be looking for at time 0,
our stock is 0-- at S0,

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:48.240 align:middle line:90%
stock goes to S1 or to S2.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:51.070 align:middle line:90%


00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:58.310 align:middle line:84%
And our value of
our call will go--

00:26:58.310 --> 00:27:02.970 align:middle line:84%
we assume that S1 is greater
than S0, S2 is less than 0,

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:10.210 align:middle line:84%
so here, value of our
option will be what?

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:18.890 align:middle line:84%
ST minus K. And value
of our call option

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:25.370 align:middle line:84%
here will be 0, because
it will be this thing.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:26.650 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:28.010 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:33.850 align:middle line:84%
So, now, what we
want to do, we want

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:36.910 align:middle line:84%
to find the
replicating portfolio.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:41.210 align:middle line:90%


00:27:41.210 --> 00:27:44.890 align:middle line:84%
And here is suggestion how--
so replicating portfolio

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:49.570 align:middle line:84%
in the case of forward
was stock itself.

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:53.330 align:middle line:90%
It was replicating the payout.

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:56.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, my suggestion is
to do the following.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:01.650 align:middle line:84%
Our replicating portfolio
would be some amount of cash

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:03.140 align:middle line:90%
and some amount of stock.

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:12.246 align:middle line:90%


00:28:12.246 --> 00:28:19.230 align:middle line:84%
And we want to find
these values of cash

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:28.270 align:middle line:84%
and how much stock we
need such that our option

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:30.920 align:middle line:90%
payoff is replicated here.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:33.774 align:middle line:90%


00:28:33.774 --> 00:28:39.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, we certainly can
do it in this simple case

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:46.990 align:middle line:84%
because, well, in the
beginning, our portfolio

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:50.910 align:middle line:90%
will be B0 plus aS0.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.590 align:middle line:90%


00:28:53.590 --> 00:29:04.830 align:middle line:84%
At the end, it will be B1
plus S1 or B2 plus aS2.

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:08.640 align:middle line:84%
And, our condition
of replication

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:11.860 align:middle line:84%
is that in this state, it
should be equal to this.

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:16.880 align:middle line:90%


00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:19.760 align:middle line:84%
And in this state, it
should be equal to this

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:21.790 align:middle line:84%
because we are
replicating our payoff.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:26.600 align:middle line:90%


00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:32.320 align:middle line:84%
And let's notice that because
our interest rate is 0, again,

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.870 align:middle line:84%
this thing doesn't change
because it's just cash.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:42.080 align:middle line:90%


00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:46.200 align:middle line:90%
Well, nice and easy.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.260 align:middle line:84%
From here, we can find
that our a-- well,

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:52.560 align:middle line:84%
if we subtract this equation
from this equation--

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.520 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we have two
equations with two unknowns.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:59.360 align:middle line:84%
So B0 is one unknown, and
the second unknown is a.

00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:02.560 align:middle line:84%
So from here, we can
easily find that a

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:07.670 align:middle line:84%
is equal S1 minus K
divided by S1 minus S2.

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:11.940 align:middle line:90%


00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:29.340 align:middle line:84%
And B0 is equal S2 times K
minus S1 divided by S1 minus S2.

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:34.580 align:middle line:84%
And, knowing that,
if we replicate it

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:38.320 align:middle line:84%
the same as before, if
we replicated the part--

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:47.020 align:middle line:84%
we have a replication portfolio,
that's where it should be here,

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:49.420 align:middle line:90%
and this is our C0.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:50.880 align:middle line:90%
And that's what we are looking.

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:55.900 align:middle line:84%
We are looking for the value
of our option at time 0.

00:30:55.900 --> 00:31:07.210 align:middle line:84%
So our C0 is equal
to aS0 minus S2.

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:10.400 align:middle line:90%


00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:30.640 align:middle line:84%
Or it is S1 minus K divided by
S1 minus S2 times S0 minus S2.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:33.480 align:middle line:90%


00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:33.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:37.680 align:middle line:84%
So basically, by the same
replication argument,

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:44.160 align:middle line:90%
we're able to find our value.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:51.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, coming back to
our risk-neutral,

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:57.840 align:middle line:84%
sure enough, if we use this
same probability, which

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:02.050 align:middle line:84%
is derived from expected
value of the stock,

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:14.570 align:middle line:84%
the price of our C0 is actually
P neutral times ST minus K

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:28.690 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus P neutral times 0.

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:33.510 align:middle line:84%
So, if we know this probability,
and we can derive it from here.

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:37.070 align:middle line:84%
Instead of doing all
this difficult machinery,

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:40.690 align:middle line:84%
we can just take
the payoff and take

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:45.410 align:middle line:84%
expected value of our payoff
using this probability

00:32:45.410 --> 00:32:48.830 align:middle line:84%
and get the value of
our derivative now.

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:52.210 align:middle line:90%


00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:52.870 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:55.530 align:middle line:90%


00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:57.360 align:middle line:90%
So that's discrete world.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:05.350 align:middle line:90%


00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:11.430 align:middle line:84%
And, yeah, so just
for your reference,

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:19.910 align:middle line:84%
here, it is the same set
of formulas for any payout.

00:33:19.910 --> 00:33:27.110 align:middle line:84%
And one other interesting,
useful comment here

00:33:27.110 --> 00:33:31.290 align:middle line:84%
is that we started with
replicating portfolios.

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:35.910 align:middle line:84%
So we started with some
cash and some stock

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:42.036 align:middle line:84%
in order to replicate the value
of our option on the next step.

00:33:42.036 --> 00:33:46.870 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand,
one can rewrite it

00:33:46.870 --> 00:33:56.590 align:middle line:84%
and say that this portfolio
is because this is just cash.

00:33:56.590 --> 00:34:00.960 align:middle line:90%
This is riskless portfolio.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:04.640 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see just in a
second that sometimes it's

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:06.520 align:middle line:84%
more convenient,
instead of talking

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.760 align:middle line:84%
about replicating
portfolios, it's

00:34:08.760 --> 00:34:12.020 align:middle line:84%
about talking the
portfolios without risk.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:16.280 align:middle line:84%
So we basically form
a portfolio of option,

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:21.000 align:middle line:84%
of full value of option, and
some stock such the combination

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:21.569 align:middle line:90%
is riskless.

00:34:21.569 --> 00:34:24.960 align:middle line:90%


00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:25.892 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:25.892 --> 00:34:29.320 align:middle line:90%
So that's our discrete world.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:35.989 align:middle line:84%
With that, let's move
on to continuous world.

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:42.120 align:middle line:90%


00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:48.639 align:middle line:84%
So, in continuous world, for
today, we'll be looking--

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:54.679 align:middle line:84%
we'll be working with
lognormal dynamics,

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:58.760 align:middle line:84%
but we'll see that it's
not absolutely crucial.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:01.460 align:middle line:84%
It's just a nice
example, once again.

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And lognormal dynamic
is basically dynamic

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:16.200 align:middle line:84%
which implies that
there is some growth.

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:18.360 align:middle line:84%
And there is some
random component.

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:22.340 align:middle line:90%


00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:28.290 align:middle line:84%
Well, one can also
write it in this form.

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:38.420 align:middle line:90%


00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:43.500 align:middle line:84%
And let's talk a little
bit about dW, what it is.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:51.140 align:middle line:84%
So dW is a random
walk linear process.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:57.880 align:middle line:84%
So expected value
of dW is equal to 0,

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:07.440 align:middle line:84%
and standard deviation of dW
is equal to square root of dt.

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:12.480 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
always bothers me.

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:16.840 align:middle line:84%
Why standard deviation has
to be square root of dt?

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:19.080 align:middle line:90%
Why do we know that?

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:24.480 align:middle line:84%
Well, and here is a hand-wavy
reason without any matter theory

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:28.920 align:middle line:84%
or anything else
is the following.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:36.760 align:middle line:90%
So suppose we have dt steps.

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:43.760 align:middle line:90%
Our random walk had n steps.

00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:56.335 align:middle line:84%
So, the collection of those
will be dW0 dW1, so on, and dWn.

00:36:56.335 --> 00:37:00.290 align:middle line:90%


00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:03.190 align:middle line:90%
And the-- not equal.

00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:07.770 align:middle line:84%
So our dWn, the final
value after n steps,

00:37:07.770 --> 00:37:10.420 align:middle line:84%
it will be sum of all
the smaller steps.

00:37:10.420 --> 00:37:16.770 align:middle line:90%


00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:27.970 align:middle line:90%
So, what's expected value of Wn?

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:38.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, it is sum of the
expected values of all dWs,

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:41.410 align:middle line:84%
and each of them
is 0, so it is 0.

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:44.650 align:middle line:84%
It's, in fact,
martingale, it stays 0.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:53.634 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is the standard
deviation, or variance,

00:37:53.634 --> 00:37:57.910 align:middle line:90%
of our dWn?

00:37:57.910 --> 00:38:05.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, it will be sum of
squared of each step.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:08.590 align:middle line:90%


00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:14.870 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and what I forgot
to say-- to tell here,

00:38:14.870 --> 00:38:25.070 align:middle line:84%
is that probability
of us going up--

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:27.990 align:middle line:90%


00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:31.180 align:middle line:84%
we are going by 1,
either up or down.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:38.070 align:middle line:90%


00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:43.670 align:middle line:90%
And on each step, it is 1/2.

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:47.150 align:middle line:84%
With probability 1/2,
we go up or down.

00:38:47.150 --> 00:39:05.700 align:middle line:84%
And correlation, our
steps are not correlated.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:07.680 align:middle line:90%
That's important.

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:21.600 align:middle line:84%
So then the variance here will
be sum of expected values of dWi

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:29.880 align:middle line:84%
squared, each of them will
be 1 with probability 1/2.

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:40.680 align:middle line:90%
So it will be n.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:43.680 align:middle line:84%
So with probability
1/2, it is 1.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:46.220 align:middle line:84%
With probability
1/2, it's minus 1.

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:54.980 align:middle line:84%
So sum of squares will be, well,
1, so n steps, it will be n.

00:39:54.980 --> 00:39:56.340 align:middle line:90%
OK, fine.

00:39:56.340 --> 00:40:05.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's put our n steps
on a finite period of time.

00:40:05.060 --> 00:40:26.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that we have
a finite period of time

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:35.020 align:middle line:84%
and we split it at n equal
periods of small delta t.

00:40:35.020 --> 00:40:40.380 align:middle line:84%
And we also-- let's assume that
instead of doing the steps of 1

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:52.360 align:middle line:84%
and minus 1, let's assume
that there are some number d.

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:58.700 align:middle line:84%
Plus or minus d
with probability--

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:02.260 align:middle line:90%


00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:02.760 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:09.200 align:middle line:90%


00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:09.920 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:16.400 align:middle line:84%
So expected value of our
random walk still stays 0.

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:20.010 align:middle line:84%
And our variance
now becomes what?

00:41:20.010 --> 00:41:27.240 align:middle line:90%


00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:33.690 align:middle line:84%
Sum of 1/2 d squared
plus 1/2 d squared.

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:36.320 align:middle line:90%


00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:39.850 align:middle line:90%
So nd squared.

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:43.050 align:middle line:90%


00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:53.610 align:middle line:84%
And our n, we assume that it
is just split period of time.

00:41:53.610 --> 00:41:58.570 align:middle line:84%
So Td squared divided
by delta t right.

00:41:58.570 --> 00:42:00.370 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what
I'm trying to say

00:42:00.370 --> 00:42:05.290 align:middle line:84%
is that, if we put our
steps on a period of time,

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:07.230 align:middle line:90%
that's what's our variance.

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:13.610 align:middle line:84%
And now, if we make the steps
smaller, the time steps smaller,

00:42:13.610 --> 00:42:17.310 align:middle line:84%
and the space steps
smaller as well,

00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:19.190 align:middle line:84%
we're kind of taking
into infinity.

00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:25.530 align:middle line:84%
We make-- we are taking a limit
over the finite period of time,

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:31.730 align:middle line:84%
and the only way for this
to stay finite and not 0

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:40.170 align:middle line:84%
is for this to be of order of
magnitude of square root of t.

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:46.990 align:middle line:90%
So a long-winded question--

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:50.590 align:middle line:90%


00:42:50.590 --> 00:43:00.950 align:middle line:84%
a long-winded answer is,
for why standard deviation

00:43:00.950 --> 00:43:04.050 align:middle line:84%
of our Brownian motion has
to be square root of t.

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:08.870 align:middle line:84%
Because otherwise, we either
will have zero variance

00:43:08.870 --> 00:43:10.590 align:middle line:90%
or exploding process.

00:43:10.590 --> 00:43:17.190 align:middle line:84%
So for this process not to
explode, this has to be true.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:19.270 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:43:19.270 --> 00:43:25.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, another observation
before we go to option pricing

00:43:25.750 --> 00:43:35.710 align:middle line:84%
is to talk about Taylor formula
and Ito formula as the follow-up

00:43:35.710 --> 00:43:36.370 align:middle line:90%
to it.

00:43:36.370 --> 00:43:42.272 align:middle line:90%


00:43:42.272 --> 00:43:47.840 align:middle line:84%
So, if our life
was deterministic

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:58.200 align:middle line:84%
and there was a function
of two variables, S and t,

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:02.120 align:middle line:84%
then our Taylor formula
to the first degree--

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:17.120 align:middle line:84%
or to the second degree,
let's write it down,

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:20.680 align:middle line:84%
we'll take the
partial derivatives

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:26.100 align:middle line:84%
with respect of both, and then
we'll have second derivatives.

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:45.310 align:middle line:84%
So 1/2 of d 2 f by dt squared
dt squared plus 1/2 d 2 f by ds

00:44:45.310 --> 00:44:45.810 align:middle line:90%
squared.

00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:49.620 align:middle line:90%


00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:53.080 align:middle line:90%
Plus mixed derivative.

00:44:53.080 --> 00:45:06.100 align:middle line:90%


00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:11.700 align:middle line:84%
So this is to second
degree plus delta.

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:12.960 align:middle line:90%
This is to the second degree.

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:19.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, in our stochastic
world, these two terms

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:22.620 align:middle line:90%
have different magnitude.

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:27.460 align:middle line:84%
So the deterministic
term is of magnitude dt,

00:45:27.460 --> 00:45:33.740 align:middle line:84%
and stochastic term is of
magnitude of square root.

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:39.840 align:middle line:84%
So, if we go back to
our Taylor formula

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:45.260 align:middle line:84%
and would like to have
expansion to the first degree,

00:45:45.260 --> 00:45:48.360 align:middle line:90%
what will have to do?

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:50.030 align:middle line:90%
So in our stochastic world--

00:45:50.030 --> 00:46:00.080 align:middle line:90%


00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:02.100 align:middle line:90%
so this is the first degree.

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:03.220 align:middle line:90%
So this we keep.

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:07.720 align:middle line:90%


00:46:07.720 --> 00:46:15.740 align:middle line:84%
This is first degree, or
first degree or less right

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:18.960 align:middle line:90%
because our dS is dt plus--

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.760 align:middle line:84%
this is of order of
magnitude of square root dt.

00:46:22.760 --> 00:46:26.226 align:middle line:90%
So this we'll keep completely.

00:46:26.226 --> 00:46:30.400 align:middle line:90%


00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:37.290 align:middle line:84%
And now if we go to mixed terms,
this is second degree squarely.

00:46:37.290 --> 00:46:38.770 align:middle line:90%
Get rid of it.

00:46:38.770 --> 00:46:46.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is certainly more than
first degree, so get rid of it.

00:46:46.270 --> 00:46:49.010 align:middle line:90%
But here, something is left.

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:53.630 align:middle line:84%
And what is left is square
of this, roughly speaking.

00:46:53.630 --> 00:47:03.450 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have to have a term
which is coming from dS squared,

00:47:03.450 --> 00:47:11.000 align:middle line:84%
which will be 1/2 of
d 2 f by ds squared.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:13.730 align:middle line:90%


00:47:13.730 --> 00:47:17.170 align:middle line:84%
And from ds squared,
the first term

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:20.230 align:middle line:84%
will be dt squared,
not interesting.

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:27.690 align:middle line:84%
The mixed term will be dt
times dW will be less than dt,

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:31.710 align:middle line:84%
but the square of
dW will be exactly

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:34.130 align:middle line:90%
of the order of magnitude of dt.

00:47:34.130 --> 00:47:40.380 align:middle line:84%
So here will be sigma
squared S squared dt--

00:47:40.380 --> 00:47:41.910 align:middle line:90%
dS squared, sorry.

00:47:41.910 --> 00:47:46.790 align:middle line:90%


00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:50.350 align:middle line:84%
And this is called
Ito's formula.

00:47:50.350 --> 00:47:54.870 align:middle line:84%
Very important for
stochastic calculus.

00:47:54.870 --> 00:48:01.750 align:middle line:84%
And this is quick and
dirty derivation of it.

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:02.630 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:14.390 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's move on to our--

00:48:14.390 --> 00:48:17.260 align:middle line:90%
now this will leave probably.

00:48:17.260 --> 00:48:20.190 align:middle line:90%


00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:25.050 align:middle line:84%
So we'll need this, this,
but this we don't need.

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:29.280 align:middle line:90%


00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:31.710 align:middle line:90%
So let's move to pricing.

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:38.320 align:middle line:90%


00:48:38.320 --> 00:48:49.560 align:middle line:84%
So we still want to price
some claim, derivative claim.

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:54.240 align:middle line:90%
And we'll call it f in our case.

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:56.980 align:middle line:84%
And we'll form a
replicating portfolio.

00:48:56.980 --> 00:49:09.680 align:middle line:90%


00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:12.520 align:middle line:84%
And as before, our
replicating portfolio

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:16.890 align:middle line:84%
will consist of some
stock, some cash.

00:49:16.890 --> 00:49:19.920 align:middle line:90%


00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And what we want to do is to
replicate our value of our claim

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:27.520 align:middle line:90%
on each step, on each step dt.

00:49:27.520 --> 00:49:32.900 align:middle line:90%


00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:37.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's do the trick
which we did before.

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:38.600 align:middle line:90%
Let's rearrange this.

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:52.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's notice that aSt minus
f of t-- so the combination--

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:53.880 align:middle line:90%
or f minus a--

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:54.380 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:59.564 align:middle line:90%


00:49:59.564 --> 00:50:05.340 align:middle line:84%
That this combination of
our derivative and stock

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:05.930 align:middle line:90%
is riskless.

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:09.300 align:middle line:90%


00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:11.420 align:middle line:90%
And what does it mean?

00:50:11.420 --> 00:50:20.260 align:middle line:84%
That-- well, now, we'll assume
that interest rates are not 0,

00:50:20.260 --> 00:50:21.120 align:middle line:90%
what does it mean?

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:24.920 align:middle line:84%
It means that this
thing, if it's riskless,

00:50:24.920 --> 00:50:32.200 align:middle line:84%
it has to grow with
time as e to the rt.

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:33.340 align:middle line:90%
By definition.

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:45.000 align:middle line:84%
So Bt is equal e
to the rt times B0.

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:48.280 align:middle line:84%
So we started our
strategy at time 0.

00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:56.360 align:middle line:84%
And since then, our cash is
growing with interest rate t.

00:50:56.360 --> 00:50:57.920 align:middle line:90%
And what does this mean?

00:50:57.920 --> 00:51:05.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, this means that our dB
is deterministic function.

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:10.000 align:middle line:84%
No need for Ito or
anything like this.

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:16.560 align:middle line:84%
And we all can
differentiate exponentials.

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:21.320 align:middle line:90%


00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:23.080 align:middle line:90%
So trivial mass.

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:25.410 align:middle line:90%
That's how rB works.

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:30.290 align:middle line:84%
So, now, that's-- so let's
try to differentiate the whole

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:30.790 align:middle line:90%
thing.

00:51:30.790 --> 00:51:34.070 align:middle line:84%
We know the right side,
because it's deterministic.

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:36.610 align:middle line:90%
It will be this.

00:51:36.610 --> 00:51:51.050 align:middle line:84%
So, the derivative of
our riskless portfolio

00:51:51.050 --> 00:51:59.850 align:middle line:84%
will be r times our
riskless portfolio times dt.

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:03.890 align:middle line:90%


00:52:03.890 --> 00:52:04.690 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:10.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, we'll keep it as a
right-hand side, and for this,

00:52:10.490 --> 00:52:11.915 align:middle line:90%
we'll use our--

00:52:11.915 --> 00:52:15.330 align:middle line:90%


00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:17.570 align:middle line:90%
either Ito's formula.

00:52:17.570 --> 00:52:23.590 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's do that.

00:52:23.590 --> 00:52:35.510 align:middle line:84%
So we continue to differentiate
that, and we get df minus a dS.

00:52:35.510 --> 00:52:40.830 align:middle line:84%
And our f is stochastic,
we use our Ito's formula,

00:52:40.830 --> 00:52:58.630 align:middle line:84%
would be d of dt times dt
plus df dS times dS plus

00:52:58.630 --> 00:53:10.120 align:middle line:84%
our stochastic term, plus 1/2
d 2 f by dS squared dS squared.

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:14.710 align:middle line:90%


00:53:14.710 --> 00:53:19.240 align:middle line:84%
And we don't forget
to subtract this.

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:22.090 align:middle line:90%


00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:30.050 align:middle line:84%
And if we just continue further
and start collecting terms--

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:35.830 align:middle line:84%
so we'll expand our dS
according to our formula.

00:53:35.830 --> 00:53:39.970 align:middle line:84%
And we'll collect terms
with the certain terms

00:53:39.970 --> 00:53:44.230 align:middle line:84%
with dt and uncertain
terms with our dW.

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:47.850 align:middle line:84%
So, if we collecting
our terms with dt here,

00:53:47.850 --> 00:54:06.170 align:middle line:84%
it will be df by dt plus
1/2 coming from dS squared,

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:11.890 align:middle line:84%
and dS squared, remember, it
will be sigma squared S squared

00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:12.770 align:middle line:90%
dt.

00:54:12.770 --> 00:54:15.970 align:middle line:90%
It comes from this only.

00:54:15.970 --> 00:54:19.460 align:middle line:90%
So it will be--

00:54:19.460 --> 00:54:21.620 align:middle line:84%
oh, sorry, sorry,
sorry, sorry, sorry.

00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:23.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, yeah, not--
OK, that's fine.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:34.160 align:middle line:84%
Plus 1/2 d 2 f by dS squared
sigma squared S squared.

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:36.260 align:middle line:90%
And I missed this one.

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:40.580 align:middle line:84%
And if we are collecting the
dt terms, it has to be that.

00:54:40.580 --> 00:54:43.420 align:middle line:90%


00:54:43.420 --> 00:54:54.100 align:middle line:84%
Plus df by dS times mu
S. And we should not

00:54:54.100 --> 00:55:04.500 align:middle line:84%
forget dt term from
here minus a mu S,

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:08.620 align:middle line:90%
and that completes our dt term.

00:55:08.620 --> 00:55:16.580 align:middle line:84%
Now let's see what
happens with our dW term.

00:55:16.580 --> 00:55:21.012 align:middle line:84%
Well, dW term will be
partially from here.

00:55:21.012 --> 00:55:27.960 align:middle line:90%
df by dS sigma S from here.

00:55:27.960 --> 00:55:30.960 align:middle line:90%


00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:40.360 align:middle line:84%
Minus part from here, a sigma
S. And this is equal dW--

00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:41.260 align:middle line:90%
times dW.

00:55:41.260 --> 00:55:43.860 align:middle line:90%
And what it is equal to?

00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:46.800 align:middle line:90%
Well, it is equal to this.

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:48.440 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:55:48.440 --> 00:55:51.505 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: How come we can say
that dW squared equals dt?

00:55:51.505 --> 00:55:53.880 align:middle line:84%
Because I thought we just knew
that standard deviation is

00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:55.960 align:middle line:84%
the square root
of dt, but, like--

00:55:55.960 --> 00:55:59.384 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: So
dW squared is dt.

00:55:59.384 --> 00:56:01.140 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: But I
thought W was random.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:02.380 align:middle line:90%
I thought dW was random.

00:56:02.380 --> 00:56:04.172 align:middle line:84%
So how can you say that
when you square it,

00:56:04.172 --> 00:56:06.720 align:middle line:90%
you get something deterministic?

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:12.800 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: Well, this
is more of a notation,

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:21.370 align:middle line:84%
but yeah, we kind of-- yeah, it
is not very rigorous notation.

00:56:21.370 --> 00:56:30.110 align:middle line:84%
But that's basically what
Ito's formula tells you.

00:56:30.110 --> 00:56:34.770 align:middle line:84%
So if you go into
the strict proof,

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:38.290 align:middle line:84%
that's what you will get,
basically, for the second--

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:39.810 align:middle line:90%
for the squared term.

00:56:39.810 --> 00:56:41.430 align:middle line:90%
But--

00:56:41.430 --> 00:56:44.930 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: --add to that, when
we talked about Brownian motion,

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:48.650 align:middle line:84%
the quadratic variation
of Brownian motion

00:56:48.650 --> 00:56:52.525 align:middle line:90%
is constant over time.

00:56:52.525 --> 00:56:53.150 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: I see.

00:56:53.150 --> 00:56:56.130 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And so over the
infinitesimal integrals--

00:56:56.130 --> 00:56:57.970 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That makes
sense, thanks.

00:56:57.970 --> 00:57:00.050 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: A combination
of infinitesimal,

00:57:00.050 --> 00:57:05.010 align:middle line:84%
and the fact that the-- yeah,
that the standard deviation is

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:06.390 align:middle line:90%
of order of magnitude.

00:57:06.390 --> 00:57:09.740 align:middle line:84%
But yes, you are right, it
is kind of sloppy notation.

00:57:09.740 --> 00:57:12.690 align:middle line:90%


00:57:12.690 --> 00:57:13.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:13.690 --> 00:57:27.170 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal
to rf minus raS dt.

00:57:27.170 --> 00:57:30.590 align:middle line:90%


00:57:30.590 --> 00:57:34.990 align:middle line:84%
Because we knew that this
part is deterministic.

00:57:34.990 --> 00:57:39.310 align:middle line:84%
So there are no dW
terms on this side.

00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:41.310 align:middle line:90%
So what does it mean?

00:57:41.310 --> 00:57:46.550 align:middle line:84%
That this has to
be equal to this,

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:55.590 align:middle line:84%
and this has to be equal to 0
because right-hand side here

00:57:55.590 --> 00:57:58.910 align:middle line:90%
is completely deterministic.

00:57:58.910 --> 00:58:03.070 align:middle line:90%
So uncertainty has to disappear.

00:58:03.070 --> 00:58:05.370 align:middle line:84%
Why it disappears,
it's by construction.

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Because from the
very beginning, we

00:58:08.470 --> 00:58:10.750 align:middle line:84%
said that we want
to find such an a,

00:58:10.750 --> 00:58:13.890 align:middle line:84%
and that's what we'll
get just in a second,

00:58:13.890 --> 00:58:15.440 align:middle line:90%
that this portfolio is riskless.

00:58:15.440 --> 00:58:18.170 align:middle line:90%


00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:23.670 align:middle line:84%
Or another way that we can
replicate our derivative.

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:33.460 align:middle line:84%
So, if this is equal to 0, then
our a is equal to df by dS.

00:58:33.460 --> 00:58:42.040 align:middle line:90%


00:58:42.040 --> 00:58:43.330 align:middle line:90%
And we are done.

00:58:43.330 --> 00:58:51.050 align:middle line:84%
So what we are done
in a sense, that we

00:58:51.050 --> 00:58:58.290 align:middle line:84%
arrive at the following
equation, basically.

00:58:58.290 --> 00:58:59.630 align:middle line:90%
Where should I write?

00:58:59.630 --> 00:59:00.470 align:middle line:90%
How about this?

00:59:00.470 --> 00:59:09.420 align:middle line:90%


00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:14.560 align:middle line:84%
So our main equation will
be this equal to this,

00:59:14.560 --> 00:59:16.080 align:middle line:90%
but we will substitute a.

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:19.060 align:middle line:90%


00:59:19.060 --> 00:59:25.700 align:middle line:84%
And let me write it here, the
equation which we arrive at.

00:59:25.700 --> 00:59:39.220 align:middle line:84%
df by dt plus 1/2 d 2 f by dS
squared sigma squared S squared

00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:50.740 align:middle line:84%
is equal to rf minus
rdf by dS times S.

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:55.060 align:middle line:84%
And this is the
Black-Scholes equation.

00:59:55.060 --> 00:59:57.320 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we
are looking for,

00:59:57.320 --> 01:00:01.260 align:middle line:84%
and that's what we
will be solving.

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:08.140 align:middle line:90%
So just to give--

01:00:08.140 --> 01:00:08.640 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

01:00:08.640 --> 01:00:14.400 align:middle line:84%
So in the slides, there are
nicer and more cleaner write-up.

01:00:14.400 --> 01:00:17.160 align:middle line:90%


01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:17.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:17.680 --> 01:00:26.600 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what we found
out, that any derivative claim,

01:00:26.600 --> 01:00:33.280 align:middle line:84%
any payoff has to
satisfy this equation.

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:38.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, under assumptions of our
lognormal motion, if we assume

01:00:38.200 --> 01:00:39.680 align:middle line:84%
some other dynamics
of the stock,

01:00:39.680 --> 01:00:42.360 align:middle line:84%
the equation will be different,
but we still can derive it

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:44.230 align:middle line:90%
in a similar manner.

01:00:44.230 --> 01:00:47.870 align:middle line:90%


01:00:47.870 --> 01:00:51.080 align:middle line:84%
But the beauty of it that
any derivative claim,

01:00:51.080 --> 01:00:56.400 align:middle line:84%
any payout at any
time before expiry

01:00:56.400 --> 01:01:00.260 align:middle line:90%
has to satisfy this equation.

01:01:00.260 --> 01:01:05.680 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, this equation
doesn't depend on our drift.

01:01:05.680 --> 01:01:09.730 align:middle line:90%
On our real-world drift.

01:01:09.730 --> 01:01:12.840 align:middle line:90%
And we will come back to this--

01:01:12.840 --> 01:01:13.340 align:middle line:90%
shortly.

01:01:13.340 --> 01:01:16.690 align:middle line:90%


01:01:16.690 --> 01:01:17.670 align:middle line:90%
So it doesn't matter.

01:01:17.670 --> 01:01:21.414 align:middle line:90%
What matters is volatility.

01:01:21.414 --> 01:01:24.810 align:middle line:90%
And we will touch upon--

01:01:24.810 --> 01:01:27.730 align:middle line:84%
hopefully we'll have time
to touch upon it further

01:01:27.730 --> 01:01:31.250 align:middle line:84%
in Black-Scholes theory,
it is assumed constant,

01:01:31.250 --> 01:01:33.490 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't have to be.

01:01:33.490 --> 01:01:36.010 align:middle line:84%
You can assume some
function or you

01:01:36.010 --> 01:01:38.830 align:middle line:84%
can assume this to
be stochastic itself,

01:01:38.830 --> 01:01:43.790 align:middle line:84%
which will make the equations
more complicated to solve,

01:01:43.790 --> 01:01:47.290 align:middle line:90%
but once again.

01:01:47.290 --> 01:01:53.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Fisher Black,
Myron Scholes, and Robert Merton

01:01:53.530 --> 01:01:54.730 align:middle line:90%
discovered--

01:01:54.730 --> 01:01:57.990 align:middle line:84%
or published in
their 1973 paper.

01:01:57.990 --> 01:02:01.690 align:middle line:84%
Believe it or not, it was
quite controversial because--

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:05.470 align:middle line:84%
and actually wasn't very
much applauded at the time,

01:02:05.470 --> 01:02:10.630 align:middle line:84%
but was recognized by
a Nobel Prize in '97.

01:02:10.630 --> 01:02:17.990 align:middle line:84%
And the always fun fact to
say that all three of them

01:02:17.990 --> 01:02:21.070 align:middle line:90%
are associated with MIT.

01:02:21.070 --> 01:02:25.110 align:middle line:84%
And Bob Merton is still,
I think, a professor

01:02:25.110 --> 01:02:33.470 align:middle line:84%
at MIT, which is obviously
quite nice to talk about

01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:35.710 align:middle line:90%
in these walls.

01:02:35.710 --> 01:02:42.516 align:middle line:84%
So now, coming-- well,
maybe a couple of remarks.

01:02:42.516 --> 01:02:45.910 align:middle line:90%


01:02:45.910 --> 01:02:47.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:51.190 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so which I said that--
so any derivative satisfies

01:02:51.190 --> 01:02:56.450 align:middle line:84%
this equation, it doesn't
depend on real-world drift.

01:02:56.450 --> 01:03:00.530 align:middle line:84%
We obtained this equation
by replicating strategy,

01:03:00.530 --> 01:03:03.610 align:middle line:90%
and that's the most important.

01:03:03.610 --> 01:03:07.950 align:middle line:84%
The other beauty of it is, it's
not only replicating strategy,

01:03:07.950 --> 01:03:09.510 align:middle line:90%
it's also hedging strategy.

01:03:09.510 --> 01:03:15.290 align:middle line:84%
So if we know our a, which
is actually derivative

01:03:15.290 --> 01:03:18.450 align:middle line:84%
of our current price
with respect to stock,

01:03:18.450 --> 01:03:22.410 align:middle line:90%
we can hedge out all the risk.

01:03:22.410 --> 01:03:25.050 align:middle line:84%
So basically, by holding
option and some amount

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:28.730 align:middle line:84%
of stock, this amount
of stock, our portfolio

01:03:28.730 --> 01:03:34.130 align:middle line:84%
becomes riskless, which is very
important practical conclusion.

01:03:34.130 --> 01:03:38.050 align:middle line:84%
The more mathematical
technical observations

01:03:38.050 --> 01:03:41.510 align:middle line:84%
are that this is actually
a form of heat equation,

01:03:41.510 --> 01:03:44.690 align:middle line:84%
and a good technical
exercise would

01:03:44.690 --> 01:03:50.090 align:middle line:84%
be to figure out what
change of variables

01:03:50.090 --> 01:03:52.690 align:middle line:84%
gives you the heat
equation, and this

01:03:52.690 --> 01:03:55.470 align:middle line:84%
is very well-studied
and solved problem,

01:03:55.470 --> 01:04:00.500 align:middle line:84%
even though it has
to be numerical.

01:04:00.500 --> 01:04:05.580 align:middle line:84%
So as any partial differential
equation, to solve it,

01:04:05.580 --> 01:04:10.940 align:middle line:90%
it will require the initial--

01:04:10.940 --> 01:04:14.420 align:middle line:84%
or actually, in this
case, final conditions

01:04:14.420 --> 01:04:16.260 align:middle line:90%
and boundary conditions.

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:18.240 align:middle line:90%
And what is our final condition?

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:24.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, our final condition is
actually our final payoff,

01:04:24.060 --> 01:04:25.200 align:middle line:90%
which will be this.

01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:27.100 align:middle line:90%
This is our final condition.

01:04:27.100 --> 01:04:28.780 align:middle line:90%
This we know.

01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:33.380 align:middle line:84%
And what will be our
boundary condition for call?

01:04:33.380 --> 01:04:39.100 align:middle line:84%
Will be that at 0, the
value of the call is 0.

01:04:39.100 --> 01:04:47.220 align:middle line:84%
And the other boundary condition
would be that at infinity, it

01:04:47.220 --> 01:04:49.940 align:middle line:90%
is actually the--

01:04:49.940 --> 01:04:56.860 align:middle line:90%
approaches S minus K.

01:04:56.860 --> 01:05:09.440 align:middle line:84%
And if we imply these
boundary conditions and final

01:05:09.440 --> 01:05:13.680 align:middle line:84%
conditions-- and this is
the conditions for a put,

01:05:13.680 --> 01:05:16.880 align:middle line:90%
we can derive--

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:20.240 align:middle line:84%
arrive to analytical or
semi-analytical formulas

01:05:20.240 --> 01:05:22.560 align:middle line:90%
for the prices.

01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:27.880 align:middle line:84%
Of course, it's expressed in the
terms of error function, which

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:29.220 align:middle line:90%
has to be tabulated.

01:05:29.220 --> 01:05:32.400 align:middle line:90%


01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:33.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:33.680 --> 01:05:36.920 align:middle line:84%
In more complicated
cases, in the cases

01:05:36.920 --> 01:05:41.380 align:middle line:84%
of more complicated
dynamics, of course,

01:05:41.380 --> 01:05:44.240 align:middle line:84%
the formulas are not so
nice, and most probably, it

01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:47.280 align:middle line:84%
will require some
numerical integration

01:05:47.280 --> 01:05:51.880 align:middle line:84%
and numerical procedures,
which is a big part

01:05:51.880 --> 01:05:54.160 align:middle line:90%
of numerical finance, in fact.

01:05:54.160 --> 01:05:58.010 align:middle line:84%
What I wanted to talk
about is coming back

01:05:58.010 --> 01:06:01.010 align:middle line:90%
to risk-neutral pricing.

01:06:01.010 --> 01:06:10.420 align:middle line:90%
And let me do it here.

01:06:10.420 --> 01:06:13.490 align:middle line:90%


01:06:13.490 --> 01:06:18.570 align:middle line:84%
Basically, how do we find
our risk-neutral measure?

01:06:18.570 --> 01:06:22.410 align:middle line:84%
And how do we find
our risk-neutral PDF?

01:06:22.410 --> 01:06:30.090 align:middle line:90%
Well actually-- we're fine.

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:33.530 align:middle line:84%
In the case of lognormal,
it is pretty simple

01:06:33.530 --> 01:06:53.890 align:middle line:84%
because, well, our dS is equal
to mu S dt plus sigma S dW.

01:06:53.890 --> 01:06:56.590 align:middle line:90%
So, here is a nice trick.

01:06:56.590 --> 01:07:01.710 align:middle line:84%
If we take expectation
value of both sides,

01:07:01.710 --> 01:07:04.500 align:middle line:84%
then we'll get a
differential equation for--

01:07:04.500 --> 01:07:07.870 align:middle line:90%


01:07:07.870 --> 01:07:12.690 align:middle line:84%
well, for expected
value of this is 0

01:07:12.690 --> 01:07:18.290 align:middle line:84%
because expected value
of dW is equal 0.

01:07:18.290 --> 01:07:26.190 align:middle line:84%
So an expected value
of this will be mu dt.

01:07:26.190 --> 01:07:30.470 align:middle line:90%


01:07:30.470 --> 01:07:34.910 align:middle line:84%
So, nice differential equation
for our expected value.

01:07:34.910 --> 01:07:43.150 align:middle line:84%
So our-- the expected
value of the stock

01:07:43.150 --> 01:07:51.310 align:middle line:84%
should be equal to initial
value of the stock times e

01:07:51.310 --> 01:07:53.730 align:middle line:90%
to the mu t.

01:07:53.730 --> 01:07:59.450 align:middle line:84%
But for our risk-neutral
assumptions, remember--

01:07:59.450 --> 01:08:02.770 align:middle line:84%
so we said that
in discrete case,

01:08:02.770 --> 01:08:09.970 align:middle line:84%
the whole point of risk-neutral
probability was that expected

01:08:09.970 --> 01:08:15.210 align:middle line:90%
value of our stock should be--

01:08:15.210 --> 01:08:19.689 align:middle line:84%
our stock may be compounded
by interest rate.

01:08:19.689 --> 01:08:24.649 align:middle line:84%
Well, when interest rate
was 0, it was constant.

01:08:24.649 --> 01:08:31.450 align:middle line:84%
It was-- so in lognormal
dynamics, dS over S

01:08:31.450 --> 01:08:33.590 align:middle line:90%
will be martingale.

01:08:33.590 --> 01:08:38.810 align:middle line:84%
So this has to be
equal to Se to the rt

01:08:38.810 --> 01:08:42.130 align:middle line:84%
because under
risk-neutral dynamics,

01:08:42.130 --> 01:08:46.490 align:middle line:90%
our stock has to grow with r.

01:08:46.490 --> 01:08:49.370 align:middle line:84%
So basically, it
tells us that mu

01:08:49.370 --> 01:08:56.450 align:middle line:84%
has to be equal to r under
our risk-neutral measure.

01:08:56.450 --> 01:08:58.979 align:middle line:90%


01:08:58.979 --> 01:09:07.220 align:middle line:84%
So, now, OK, mu is equal
to r, but we can easily

01:09:07.220 --> 01:09:15.060 align:middle line:84%
find a PDF of this
process at time t,

01:09:15.060 --> 01:09:18.060 align:middle line:84%
and that's what it is, of
lognormal process, basically.

01:09:18.060 --> 01:09:21.231 align:middle line:90%


01:09:21.231 --> 01:09:25.160 align:middle line:90%
With mu substituted by r.

01:09:25.160 --> 01:09:28.420 align:middle line:90%


01:09:28.420 --> 01:09:31.319 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah.

01:09:31.319 --> 01:09:34.819 align:middle line:90%
So mu substituted by r.

01:09:34.819 --> 01:09:42.660 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm saying, that
now instead of solving

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:47.819 align:middle line:84%
our Black-Scholes
equation for any claim

01:09:47.819 --> 01:09:56.200 align:middle line:84%
under lognormal dynamics, we
could just take our payoff

01:09:56.200 --> 01:09:59.840 align:middle line:84%
and integrate it against
this PDF, the final PDF,

01:09:59.840 --> 01:10:01.680 align:middle line:84%
and get the value
of the function

01:10:01.680 --> 01:10:08.240 align:middle line:84%
now as the expected payoff
under risk-neutral measure.

01:10:08.240 --> 01:10:09.880 align:middle line:84%
And that's our
risk-neutral measure.

01:10:09.880 --> 01:10:13.360 align:middle line:90%


01:10:13.360 --> 01:10:15.580 align:middle line:90%
So that's a nice benefit.

01:10:15.580 --> 01:10:22.920 align:middle line:84%
Of course, it is easier for
lognormal, for other processes.

01:10:22.920 --> 01:10:30.560 align:middle line:84%
It is not that easy, but it's
certainly an added benefit.

01:10:30.560 --> 01:10:38.480 align:middle line:84%
Another example which I want
to give you in the past--

01:10:38.480 --> 01:10:42.450 align:middle line:84%
in the last 10 minutes is
another application example.

01:10:42.450 --> 01:10:48.780 align:middle line:90%


01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:53.000 align:middle line:90%
So let's form such a portfolio.

01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:59.820 align:middle line:90%


01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:03.640 align:middle line:90%
Let's buy a call and sell a put.

01:11:03.640 --> 01:11:10.580 align:middle line:90%


01:11:10.580 --> 01:11:13.900 align:middle line:90%
So what is it?

01:11:13.900 --> 01:11:17.960 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's draw a diagram.

01:11:17.960 --> 01:11:21.700 align:middle line:84%
And let's draw a diagram
at time maturity t.

01:11:21.700 --> 01:11:25.980 align:middle line:84%
So we long a call
so with some strike

01:11:25.980 --> 01:11:32.500 align:middle line:84%
K. So this is our strike K.
So our call looks like this.

01:11:32.500 --> 01:11:34.860 align:middle line:90%
We short a put.

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:39.220 align:middle line:84%
So put would be like this, and
short put, it we'll flip it,

01:11:39.220 --> 01:11:42.220 align:middle line:90%
so it's like this.

01:11:42.220 --> 01:11:45.500 align:middle line:90%
How does it look?

01:11:45.500 --> 01:11:50.350 align:middle line:84%
It looks like just
like a forward.

01:11:50.350 --> 01:11:58.330 align:middle line:84%
So, at maturity, this
portfolio has zero uncertainty,

01:11:58.330 --> 01:12:01.150 align:middle line:90%
it's forward.

01:12:01.150 --> 01:12:05.430 align:middle line:84%
So at any time
before maturity, it

01:12:05.430 --> 01:12:10.030 align:middle line:84%
has to be the forward
value of the stock.

01:12:10.030 --> 01:12:12.790 align:middle line:90%
So it always has to be.

01:12:12.790 --> 01:12:20.790 align:middle line:84%
So at maturity, it
is St minus K. So--

01:12:20.790 --> 01:12:22.830 align:middle line:90%
at maturity.

01:12:22.830 --> 01:12:31.790 align:middle line:84%
So at any point of time, the
difference of call and put

01:12:31.790 --> 01:12:37.110 align:middle line:84%
has to be the
difference of value

01:12:37.110 --> 01:12:39.430 align:middle line:84%
of the current
value of the stock

01:12:39.430 --> 01:12:43.220 align:middle line:84%
and, to tell the truth,
discounted value of the strike.

01:12:43.220 --> 01:12:46.900 align:middle line:90%


01:12:46.900 --> 01:12:48.150 align:middle line:90%
T minus t.

01:12:48.150 --> 01:12:52.330 align:middle line:90%


01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:56.090 align:middle line:84%
And the beauty of it that
we made zero assumptions

01:12:56.090 --> 01:13:00.970 align:middle line:84%
about dynamics of
the stock here.

01:13:00.970 --> 01:13:02.470 align:middle line:90%
This has to be true always.

01:13:02.470 --> 01:13:06.930 align:middle line:84%
It's just how the
payoffs look like.

01:13:06.930 --> 01:13:08.810 align:middle line:84%
Because we always
know forward, it

01:13:08.810 --> 01:13:12.790 align:middle line:84%
is only dependent
on interest rate.

01:13:12.790 --> 01:13:16.370 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend on
dynamics of the stock.

01:13:16.370 --> 01:13:18.650 align:middle line:84%
And this portfolio
doesn't depend

01:13:18.650 --> 01:13:20.650 align:middle line:90%
on the dynamics of the stock.

01:13:20.650 --> 01:13:24.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's quite powerful
because it basically tells you

01:13:24.250 --> 01:13:27.350 align:middle line:84%
that if this is different
at any point of time,

01:13:27.350 --> 01:13:29.410 align:middle line:90%
you have an arbitrage.

01:13:29.410 --> 01:13:32.830 align:middle line:84%
And to illustrate
that, I actually--

01:13:32.830 --> 01:13:36.370 align:middle line:90%


01:13:36.370 --> 01:13:39.570 align:middle line:90%
when-- sorry.

01:13:39.570 --> 01:13:46.340 align:middle line:84%
Went to Bloomberg, took real
prizes of calls and puts--

01:13:46.340 --> 01:13:52.180 align:middle line:84%
this is Apple stock a
while ago, admittedly.

01:13:52.180 --> 01:13:59.380 align:middle line:84%
And here, I literally plotted
the data from here for a call

01:13:59.380 --> 01:14:01.880 align:middle line:84%
and for put and
literally summed it up.

01:14:01.880 --> 01:14:06.180 align:middle line:84%
And you see that it
is a straight line.

01:14:06.180 --> 01:14:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And no surprise, because
if it wouldn't be,

01:14:10.100 --> 01:14:13.580 align:middle line:84%
it would be an arbitrage, people
will jump on it immediately.

01:14:13.580 --> 01:14:16.780 align:middle line:84%
People check for
it all the time.

01:14:16.780 --> 01:14:20.740 align:middle line:84%
The beauty of it, that
the dynamics of the stock

01:14:20.740 --> 01:14:24.080 align:middle line:84%
is not lognormal, and
how can we see it?

01:14:24.080 --> 01:14:28.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, I also plotted here
implied volatility for--

01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:31.480 align:middle line:90%
I think I did it for call here.

01:14:31.480 --> 01:14:33.580 align:middle line:84%
And if it would be
lognormal, the volatility

01:14:33.580 --> 01:14:37.780 align:middle line:84%
should be straight
line, and it is not.

01:14:37.780 --> 01:14:40.720 align:middle line:84%
That's why we need
more complicated models

01:14:40.720 --> 01:14:42.620 align:middle line:90%
for stock dynamics.

01:14:42.620 --> 01:14:45.720 align:middle line:84%
That's why Black-Scholes
is a nice example,

01:14:45.720 --> 01:14:51.420 align:middle line:84%
but we need stochastic
volatility or others.

01:14:51.420 --> 01:14:55.360 align:middle line:84%
Jump diffusion is, by
the way, a tricky thing

01:14:55.360 --> 01:15:00.460 align:middle line:84%
because the martingales with
jumps don't really work,

01:15:00.460 --> 01:15:05.880 align:middle line:84%
so life becomes
more complicated.

01:15:05.880 --> 01:15:08.700 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah.

01:15:08.700 --> 01:15:11.160 align:middle line:90%
So that's a nice--

01:15:11.160 --> 01:15:14.200 align:middle line:84%
another nice example
of replication.

01:15:14.200 --> 01:15:19.400 align:middle line:84%
And in general, that's how
a lot of things function.

01:15:19.400 --> 01:15:23.320 align:middle line:84%
What you try to do, you look
at the payout of a portfolio

01:15:23.320 --> 01:15:26.560 align:middle line:84%
and you try to replicate the
payout with the instruments

01:15:26.560 --> 01:15:29.320 align:middle line:84%
which you have at hand,
or at least approximate

01:15:29.320 --> 01:15:34.320 align:middle line:84%
the payout, because if you did
it, you know the price now.

01:15:34.320 --> 01:15:36.400 align:middle line:90%
Or you know arbitrage.

01:15:36.400 --> 01:15:43.460 align:middle line:84%
The other way to do it is form
some complicated portfolio

01:15:43.460 --> 01:15:45.820 align:middle line:90%
and find misplacement.

01:15:45.820 --> 01:15:49.380 align:middle line:90%
And then it becomes arbitrage.

01:15:49.380 --> 01:15:51.880 align:middle line:84%
Nowadays, it's
practically impossible,

01:15:51.880 --> 01:15:58.660 align:middle line:84%
but people did a lot of things
with more complicated options

01:15:58.660 --> 01:16:03.180 align:middle line:84%
even 15, 20 years--
well, 25 years ago.

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:08.620 align:middle line:84%
I think Jake has a good
story about that, how

01:16:08.620 --> 01:16:11.560 align:middle line:90%
he was starting in FX, options.

01:16:11.560 --> 01:16:13.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, just another illustration.

01:16:13.480 --> 01:16:16.780 align:middle line:84%
I did the same for IBM stock
and, yeah, sure enough,

01:16:16.780 --> 01:16:18.400 align:middle line:90%
it's the same straight line.

01:16:18.400 --> 01:16:21.790 align:middle line:84%
I didn't plot the implied vol,
but it's probably even uglier

01:16:21.790 --> 01:16:22.290 align:middle line:90%
here.

01:16:22.290 --> 01:16:25.900 align:middle line:90%


01:16:25.900 --> 01:16:32.140 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we're about at time,
but I would leave you

01:16:32.140 --> 01:16:37.590 align:middle line:84%
with a little bit of
food for thought here.

01:16:37.590 --> 01:16:40.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is called
digital option.

01:16:40.690 --> 01:16:44.890 align:middle line:84%
The payout of digital option,
or all-or-nothing option.

01:16:44.890 --> 01:16:50.550 align:middle line:84%
It's basically either pays you
0 if you end up out of the money

01:16:50.550 --> 01:16:54.060 align:middle line:84%
or it pays you 1 if you
end up in the money.

01:16:54.060 --> 01:17:00.250 align:middle line:90%


01:17:00.250 --> 01:17:03.170 align:middle line:84%
This is how Black-Scholes
would price it.

01:17:03.170 --> 01:17:08.870 align:middle line:84%
This is actually how call spread
looks from the data which I just

01:17:08.870 --> 01:17:10.910 align:middle line:90%
showed you.

01:17:10.910 --> 01:17:12.810 align:middle line:90%
Pretty close to this.

01:17:12.810 --> 01:17:18.430 align:middle line:84%
So my challenge to you is
find a replicating portfolio

01:17:18.430 --> 01:17:22.150 align:middle line:84%
which prices a digital
because I claim

01:17:22.150 --> 01:17:25.110 align:middle line:84%
that you don't need to
have a dynamic of the stock

01:17:25.110 --> 01:17:28.070 align:middle line:84%
or do any complicated
things here.

01:17:28.070 --> 01:17:33.830 align:middle line:84%
You can replicate this
payout practically exactly,

01:17:33.830 --> 01:17:37.210 align:middle line:90%
actually approximate the payout.

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:41.640 align:middle line:84%
And that's actually how
people hedge digital options.

01:17:41.640 --> 01:17:45.090 align:middle line:90%


01:17:45.090 --> 01:17:49.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so think about it.

01:17:49.130 --> 01:17:54.530 align:middle line:84%
For with this, let me stop
here, and any questions?

01:17:54.530 --> 01:17:55.540 align:middle line:90%
Happy to answer.

01:17:55.540 --> 01:18:04.090 align:middle line:90%


01:18:04.090 --> 01:18:10.890 align:middle line:84%
Looking forward a little
bit, so we have two classes,

01:18:10.890 --> 01:18:12.500 align:middle line:90%
two invited lectures.

01:18:12.500 --> 01:18:16.490 align:middle line:90%


01:18:16.490 --> 01:18:23.570 align:middle line:84%
And one of them, the one in a
week time will be by John Hull.

01:18:23.570 --> 01:18:29.530 align:middle line:84%
It will be by Zoom,
and he will be

01:18:29.530 --> 01:18:33.440 align:middle line:90%
talking about machine learning.

01:18:33.440 --> 01:18:36.360 align:middle line:84%
And I think it will be
an interesting lecture--

01:18:36.360 --> 01:18:39.040 align:middle line:84%
well, at least interesting
because for me,

01:18:39.040 --> 01:18:41.380 align:middle line:90%
John Hull is an icon.

01:18:41.380 --> 01:18:46.100 align:middle line:84%
I mean, not only he wrote the
most famous derivatives pricing

01:18:46.100 --> 01:18:51.580 align:middle line:84%
book, he also created
Hull-White formulas

01:18:51.580 --> 01:18:56.186 align:middle line:84%
and made a lot of contribution
to quantitative finance.

01:18:56.186 --> 01:19:01.920 align:middle line:84%
And we are working on a couple
of collaboration projects.

01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:03.640 align:middle line:84%
He is in the
University of Toronto,

01:19:03.640 --> 01:19:06.260 align:middle line:84%
he is a professor of
University of Toronto.

01:19:06.260 --> 01:19:09.860 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, a little
bit of advertisement.

01:19:09.860 --> 01:19:12.330 align:middle line:90%
All right, thank you very much.

01:19:12.330 --> 01:19:32.000 align:middle line:90%